1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,662
Three ways to compute a dot product over 1,000,000 elements, measured on this machine.

2
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:10,263
Plain Python pays interpreter overhead on every element.

3
00:00:10,263 --> 00:00:14,444
A million elements means a million trips through the interpreter.

4
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:21,088
The benchmark writes its timings to a result file, and everything you see is generated from

5
00:00:21,088 --> 00:00:21,708
that file.

6
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:24,575
Two commands reproduce it.

7
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:34,667
The plain loop takes 40.96 milliseconds, the generator 32.22, and the compiled dot product 0.12.

8
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:41,473
That is 341.3 times faster, with the same answer up to rounding.

9
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:44,805
The gap holds as the input grows.

10
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:50,844
These are single machine timings, so expect different absolute numbers on yours.

11
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:56,916
Point the toolkit at your own result files to get a README and a video like this one.

